There are loads of TODO/XXX items in the source, grep them!


GENERAL
-------

Add support for "required" field in the JSON file.

For later, write an event-driven scheduler.

I'm seriously thinking about slowling "porting" valkyrie to C++... Mmm.

Speed: the frames are held at 60 per second (-u lifts the limit), but the
heavy scenes do not all reach it: about 126 frames per second in PODRACE,
102 in SGNASCAR, 55 in VON4, 53 in AIRTRIX, 32 in BRAVEFF and 28 in PHARRIER
on a Ryzen 5 5600X. What costs, in that order: the translucent layers (two
passes over every translucent mesh for each layer: 25000 to 42000 draws a
frame in BRAVEFF and PHARRIER); the two SH-4 (1 to 2 million instructions
run a frame, about 3 ns each, 1 to 5 ms a frame); the two ARM7. The main
loop of the sound driver turns 312 times a frame and an interrupt comes every
22 samples: the turns in between are counted without being run
(src/odin/arm7.cpp), except with the drivers that count their turns at 0x40
of the wave memory (VON4, BRAVEFF, PHARRIER), whose turns are never the same.


HIKARU
------

Move the slave GPU access to hikaru-gpu.c. Proper support may require the
introduction of a new vk_device_accessors_t class.

MIE LLE (src/odin/mie.cpp, ODIN_MIE=lle): HACK in src/odin/mie_bridge.cpp,
port E bits 0-3 read high while the port is an output; the BOOTROM waits
for that at 0x0C00B860 and MAME reads 0xFF there. Measure the pins. The
LLE path is the default; drop the HLE stand-in in hikaru-mie.c once the
JVS inputs (players, coins, analog) are wired to the real board.
Ports 0x40/0x60/0x80/0x81, touched by the mask ROM init, are logged as
unknown. The serial adapter block (ports 0x09-0x0E) is modelled from
the Z80 code von4 uploads; its reply delays are HEURISTIC (mie.cpp
SERIAL_SEND_CYCLES, SERIAL_REQUEST_CYCLES), measure them on a board.
The CRP-1231 card reader on that line (src/odin/card_reader.cpp,
ODIN_CARD=none|reader) is an HLE stand-in: the LLE path is an H8/300H
core running the dumped firmware (MAME wangmid, crp1231lr10_ver0110.ic2).
Status byte st1 'V' that von4 tests on command 0x33 is not understood.

AICA (src/odin/aica.cpp + arm7.cpp): control block, timers, interrupts,
DMA, RTC and the ARM7 are in; still missing: the 64 slots and the DSP
(sound output), the nested-FIQ question (does reading 0x2D00 drop the
line?), and the HACK in hikaru-aica.c (page offset 0 reads 4 for
Pharrier). The ARM7 runs on the 22.5792 MHz bus clock, eight times MAME's
figure: the SGNASCAR cabinet shows its sound driver ready within a frame
of each reload, 20 MHz at least (HEURISTIC: no wait state). It costs a
quarter of the speed: the driver sleeps most of the time, its wait could
be billed without being run.

ROM board 315-5881 (src/odin/crypt_315_5881.cpp, port of MAME, validated
on the real self-test of Planet Harriers and 19.7 M words against MAME's
code): RAM at local bus 0x0A000000, registers at 0x0A010000, keys from
MAME (airtrix, pharrier, swracer). Its ready bit is never busy (MAME).
The BOOTROM board probe answers a constant for the boards without the
chip, braveff and von4 (hikaru-memctl.c, HACK until those boards are
measured).

SGNASCAR ROM board security FPGA (src/odin/actel_m1.cpp, the stream
decoder of MAME's Naomi M1 board): registers at local bus 0x0A000000, key
from MAME. Proven by the game: it reads 208 bytes at each change of scene,
compares them with its own copy and, at each mismatch, adds 20 to the
weight of the previous picture in the 181 blend. HEURISTIC: every start
reads from the first byte of the mask ROMs (no address register is known),
the ready flag is on bits 31 and 15 of the status, the other bits of the
control and of the status are not known.

GPU textures: the 0D1 UV offsets are added to the vertex UVs the way the
X360 port's vertex shader is assumed to (the Xenos microcode of vo3d was
not decoded: the sign is a HEURISTIC). Texhead filter mode 3 is trilinear
here while the X360 port leaves the sampler defaults (HEURISTIC). The
texel combine field of the 2C1 (bits 31:29) keeps the previous mode in
the port for the values 3, 6 and 7 and means plain texturing here
(HEURISTIC).
The mipmap levels of a texture alternate between the two texture banks,
as Valkyrie had it, where the X360 port keeps them in the texhead bank:
the pages BRAVEFF writes to the texture RAM itself hold the half-size
copy of each bank 0 texture in bank 1, and PODRACE keeps the textures of
the Watto shot of its intro where the level 1 of the same bank would
land (they stay intact on the cabinet).
The texture format 2 holds one 4-bit value a texel, a word being a block
of 2 x 2 texels (the BOOTROM font, 64 x 64 words for 128 x 128 texels);
the four channels get the value (HEURISTIC: the ramp of the PHARRIER
halo, 0 to F, is used for its alpha).
A texture transfer (IDMA) runs to its end whatever is written to its
control register, which reads busy until then (HEURISTIC, from the way
the library polls it; the boot ROM clears the register at a later
interrupt, in the middle of the next transfer of SGNASCAR).
The 2D layers cover the 3D where a pixel has its alpha bit (black
included: the bands of the Racer attract) and are transparent elsewhere
(its green 0x03E0 boxes) and layer 1 sits above layer 2, as the Racer
cabinet footage shows. The bit (bank - 1) of the unit register 3C turns
this keying on; without it the layer covers everything (the white SEGA
screen of von4 on the cabinet). Read from two cabinets only (HEURISTIC).
A layer whose bit of the unit register 38 is clear sits below the 3D,
which shows it where nothing was drawn when bit 0 of the 3C is set and
the clear colour has no alpha bit (the AIRTRIX logo and title screens).
Read from the cabinet footage and the registers of five games
(HEURISTIC).

GPU lighting follows the X360 port's vo3d shaders: the 691 colour is the
material ambient (255 is 1.0 and, unlike the port, a larger value is
brighter: on the PHARRIER cabinet the syringe of the nurse, emission 600
in the shading mode 2, and the selection frame of the shop, ambient 736
in the mode 1, are that much brighter; no other game goes above 255), the
091 colour stands for the texel of untextured polygons, the 491 colour is the specular colour,
the light colour drives both the diffuse and the specular terms,
light vectors arrive in eye space (the library transforms them on the
SH-4), the 881 alpha bit turns
the vertex alpha into a blend factor between 091 and 291 (or the texel),
and the 451 carries the spot
cone (cos outer in 1.15, scale as an 8-bit float; the B61 vector aims it),
faded as sat ((L . axis - cos outer) * scale). The scale is the field
read as (1 + mantissa / 16) * 2^exponent (HEURISTIC: the library builds
it as an approximate reciprocal of cos inner - cos outer by subtracting
float bit patterns; with the exact reciprocal the hall-lit scenes of
AIRTRIX are 8 to 12 % darker than the cabinet, 2 to 5 % this way; the
wood itself is still 7 to 12 % under).
The back of a polygon drawn on both faces (vertex bit 23) is lit with the
reversed normal (HEURISTIC, read from two cabinets: the SEGA logo of
PODRACE is as bright from behind as from the front and dark when its
back turns away from the light, which rules out the absolute value of
N . L; the inner faces of the PHARRIER letters). The back is told by the
winding: turning the normals toward the eye instead darkens the veils of
the PHARRIER galaxy, which the cabinet shows as bright as here. The X360
port emits the second face with the same normal.
Not as the port, read from the PHARRIER cabinet (HEURISTIC): the 491
colour is in 1/64 without the 8-bit wrap of the port (0x40 is 1.0), and
the specular lobe is the reflected ray against the view, raised to the
shininess byte, where the port takes the Blinn half vector (the SEGA
logo seen from the front stays blue, the canopy of the helicopter gets
its highlight).
Not as the port: the attenuation types 2 and 3 fall off on 1 / d and
1 / d^2 with the P and Q of the library (the port does not attenuate
them); the PHARRIER cabinet shows its asteroids untouched by the far
type 3 light of the logo scene.
A parallel light sent with a null direction and no attenuation lights
every side fully (HEURISTIC, read from the BRAVEFF cabinet, whose stages
add such a light, of colour 84 71 71, to every lightset: without it the
control room, the how to play rooms, the demo play and the wall of the
ranking are too dark). Weighing it by the material ambient (691), 0x7F in
the control room, leaves that room too dark whatever the capture curve:
the cabinet frame has more bright pixels than such a render.
The bits 19:16 of the 051 are the ambient level of the light, 0 to 15:
the light editor of PHARRIER shows them as "amb" next to the colour and
SGNASCAR builds them as (int) (16 * level). The light adds level / 16 of
its colour to every polygon it reaches, facing it or not, without the
material ambient, cut by the distance and by the cone like the Lambert
term (HEURISTIC for the scale and the two cuts, read from four cabinets:
with a level of 4 the track of SGNASCAR and its walls turned away from
the sun all gain 0.24 of their texture; the red beacon of BRAVEFF
flashes as strongly as on the cabinet, frame by frame; the board and
the disc of the AIRTRIX how to play; the explosion flash of PHARRIER.
Without the cut by the distance BRAVEFF and PHARRIER get far too
bright). von4 leaves the level at 0 and the X360 port ignores it.
Not as the port either, whose target clamps at 8 bits: a blended colour
is weighted by its alpha before it is clamped, so a lit colour above 1
keeps its excess (HEURISTIC, read from two cabinets: the veils of the
PHARRIER galaxy burn to white and the white floor decals of AIRTRIX, at
an alpha of 0.7, look opaque).

EEPROMs: the main board 93C46 (SH-4 port A, src/odin/eeprom_bridge.cpp)
and the ROM board X76F100 (bus page 0x14, src/odin/x76f100.cpp) are
real and read by the BOOTROM; the MIE 93C46 is in the LLE MIE. It holds
the system settings of the BOOTROM (two copies of 62 bytes and a CRC,
the country in byte 5): ODIN_MIE_EEPROM=<file> loads it and writes it
back on exit. Blank, the BOOTROM falls back on Japan. The other two are
not written back to disk yet. The COUNTRY SETTING page of the BOOTROM
menu is the second routine of its EXIT line: how to reach it is not
found.

Save states: -s <frame>[,...] writes <game>-<frame>.vkstate once that
frame is on screen and -l <file> resumes from it; F4 and F1 do the same
with <game>.vkstate. A state holds the RAMs, every chip, the two 3D
buffers that the blender feeds back and the pictures on their way to the
screen: a resumed run gives the pictures and the memory of a straight one
(checked on the six games, three states each, and on three states saved
while a game loads). It is a raw dump of about 115 MB, tied to the layouts
of the build that wrote it: every item carries its size and another build
refuses the state when one differs. The MIE 93C46 comes back from the
state, not from ODIN_MIE_EEPROM.

Game patches (hikaru.c, hikaru_install_game_patches) are all off, kept
as comments: airtrix 0x0C010F9A (660-frame I/O board wait shortened),
braveff 0x0C0407D0/0x0C0D522A/0x0C05B53E (forced results, purpose
unknown), pharrier 0x0C01C322 (AICA loop), sgnascar 0x0C00BC9A (EEPROM
byte) and 0x0C0130CE (600-frame I/O board wait). The BOOTROM EEPROM
patch (0x8E6) is off too. Remove the dead code once every game has been
seen running without it. Kept as a HACK: sgnascar 0x0C00BC9A, because
the X76F100 image is the Air Trix one (MAME has no NASCAR dump) and the
BOOTROM region check answers "ERROR 7" with it.

Bridge DMA (hikaru-memctl.c): the transfer completes at the start
write, like MAME. HEURISTIC: the real 315-6154 takes time, but games
refill the source buffer right after starting a transfer (von4 model
loader at 0x0C055FD4), so it must at least run ahead of the SH-4.


SH-4
----

The two SH-4 run on the Odin core (src/odin/sh4.cpp, sh4_ops.cpp,
sh4_onchip.cpp; src/cpu/sh/sh4-odin.c puts it in the machine). It gave the
registers and the memory of the Valkyrie interpreter it replaced at every
slice of the six games. ODIN_SH4_TRACE=<prefix> writes the PC and a hash of
the registers at every slice, ODIN_SH4_STATS=1 the host time of the slices.
Tests: tests/sh4.

Speed: 2.3 to 3.6 times the interpreter. What is left is the host missing
its branch predictions, one emulated instruction in three in VON4: a
translator to host code is the next step for the slower machines.

Kept as the interpreter did, each one HEURISTIC, to make right one at a
time once the scenes are compared again:
- the FPU rounds to the nearest; the six games ask for rounding to zero
  (FPSCR.RM = 1) for nearly every instruction, and treat denormals as zero
  (FPSCR.DN), which is ignored too;
- FTRC gives 0x80000000 out of range, the SH-4 saturates; FSCA, FSRRA, FIPR
  and FTRV are computed by the host, the SH-4 has its own tables and error
  bounds; a NaN comes out as the host makes it, the SH-4 gives 0x7FBFFFFF;
- no FPU exception is raised (the slave of PHARRIER enables the division
  by zero);
- the timers count one per CPU cycle whatever TCR.TPSC says, and follow
  at the end of a slice (may be what makes graphics flash in PHARRIER);
  every mode of a performance counter counts cycles;
- a store queue write goes to its target at once, PREF sends nothing;
- RFCR and WTCSR read as port A; BCR1 resets to 0x80000000 on both CPUs;
- an interrupt source is cleared when taken; IRQ10 has the code of IRQ11;
- the DMAC moves its whole count within the write that starts it;
- one instruction is one cycle; a frame is 544 lines where the games
  program 528 (hikaru.c).

Not implemented, the emulator stops with a message: TRAPA, the exceptions
(illegal instruction, illegal delay slot, address error, FPU disable,
privilege), MAC.L with S set, a write to the cache or TLB arrays. SLEEP
stops the CPU for good. The MMU and the caches are absent: LDTLB, PREF,
OCBI, OCBP and OCBWB do nothing, MOVCA.L is a plain store. None of this is
run by the six games up to their attract loops. Every code of the software
manual is decoded; FSCA and FSRRA, which it does not list, come from the
SH7091 (MAME sh4.cpp).

A read that nothing answers gives 0 (HEURISTIC: the value of the bus is
not known; BRAVEFF reads FFFFFFFC at 0C076E48).

The clock of the AICA starts from the host clock (src/odin/aica.cpp): two
runs do not leave the same byte in the memory of AIRTRIX.


GPU / RENDERING
---------------

The projection clipping planes are probably scaled by some value, because
using the ones provided by the games as they are leads to a far too close
far clipping plane. Use 'p' to toggle a standard 90 degree FOV projection and
see things (affects all games).

Translucent polygons are blended far to near at every pixel, whatever the
triangle or the mesh they come from, without writing the depth (HEURISTIC:
the order of the hardware is not known; read from three cabinets: the
faces of the SEGA letters of the PHARRIER logo, the street lamps of
AIRTRIX, a wall of fire of BRAVEFF). Whole triangles sorted on their
centroid broke those letters. The X360 port reverses its list of objects
instead. Each layer takes two passes over the translucent meshes of the
viewport, and each mesh sets its whole state again: this is the slowest
part of the renderer, to redo when speed matters.

The 101 bias is a signed 11-bit number that moves a mesh of any type but
the background by bias / 65536 of its own depth, nearer when positive
(HEURISTIC, read from three cabinets: four times as much and a light shaft
of AIRTRIX covers the skater; the lettering of the wall and the painted
lines of the SGNASCAR track, on layers of -1024 to -256, show as on the
cabinet; the shadow of the AIRTRIX skater, opaque, comes out of the floor;
the stars of PHARRIER, at -1024, stay behind its asteroids). The unit of
the hardware is not known.

The 161 with bit 18 loads a second modelview and a vertex with bit 12 of
its header follows it (the AIRTRIX trails and skater joints); bit 19, set on
the last row by one library entry, is not understood.

Triangles are wound after the vertex order and the winding bit, as the
SGNASCAR cabinet shows; the X360 port winds them after the vertex normals,
which is the same for von4 only.

Check the framebuffer blending (instructions 781, 181) on more footage: a
2D layer as a blender input is done for the PHARRIER logo screens only.
Between the two 3D buffers the blender output is written back, which
gives the motion blur of the BRAVEFF and PHARRIER cabinets (HEURISTIC:
read from the footage, the trails of the stars).

The polygons of a list show two frames after its launch, once the next
list is launched; the clear color is laid under them on the screen, at
once (the sync routine of the game libraries waits for the end of the
analysis and of the plot; RACER cabinet at 60 frames/s: the SEGA logo two
frames on the black of the next scene, the stars gone one frame before
the layer text). HEURISTIC: the plot of a list fits in a frame whatever
its size; the 781 and the 181 act at once like the clear color; two 3D
buffers are blended when their list is drawn; the textures are those of
the frame of the launch, a frame before the plot.

Figure out the effect of mesh LOD on texturing (normally, and SGNASCAR),
disable mipmaps when appropriate (instruction 0C1 maybe).

The 012 jumps with the conditions 5 and 9 compare the bounding sphere of
the model (561 centre, 055 radius) with a size in 1/16 of a pixel
(HEURISTIC: diameter * projection distance / distance; the unit is read from
the sizes PHARRIER uses, all whole pixels, and the formula from two cabinet
scenes). The 005 tests the same sphere against the view volume of the
current viewport. The 095 compares it with two sizes: SGNASCAR draws the
model plainly on one side, skips it on the other and draws it after a 501
in between; that 501 is the 0 every mesh starts with. Straight after a 055,
with no 095, the condition D jumps when the size is at least I (HEURISTIC:
the SGNASCAR cabinet shows the rows of spectators near the camera; far away
they are too small to tell, and the condition 1 is never used this way).

The 621 sets two kinds of depth queue. With a colour (bit 17 clear) the
materials with the 881 z bit are fogged, as in the X360 port. Without one
(bit 17, two builders of the PHARRIER library, "Depth Queue Start" and
"End" in its viewport menu) the polygons that do not blend fade into what
is behind them, linearly on the eye depth from near / Q to near / Q + 1 / P,
and the background is drawn first for that. Read from the PHARRIER cabinet
(HEURISTIC): its demo play loses the scenery past 1010 units and keeps the
mountains, which the game sends as translucent polygons of alpha 0xFC;
the start scaled by 0.5 or 1.5, or the end by 0.75 or 1.5, moves the
picture away from the footage. Not known: the types 2, 4 and 6 of that queue (a density as P),
whether the punch-through polygons fade like the opaque ones (assumed so),
bit 16, and the types of the coloured queue (ignored like the X360 port).

The depth buffer holds log2 (1 + eye depth) / 24, written per pixel: the
projected depth of a near plane at 0.1 or 0.01 was too coarse (black
patches on the cars of the SGNASCAR entry list, the shadow of the AIRTRIX
combo labels drawn over them, the cover of a PODRACE engine over its fan).
The depth format of the hardware and its use of the 421 range are not
known, and the 421 depth function is not applied (HEURISTIC).

The 221 offsets are signed 16-bit values: von4 centres the view of its
lever panel (tutorial of the U.S.A. setting) on (960, -85), and the levers
land in their panel as on the cabinet. No game sends a negative X: its sign
is assumed (HEURISTIC).

Implement alpha testing (used in BRAVEFF, instruction 154).

Implement proper model caching using VBOs.


AIRTRIX
-------

Attract mode works.

Emulation wise, the biggest issue is with the player character: it is not
animated. Talking with the Demul guys, we figured out the following: the game
uploads matrix data to CMDRAM  48100000-48100E2x, along with commands, but we
don't know what device that data is for. It is written by the master SH-4 but
never reads them back, and there is strong evidence that the slave SH-4 does
not touch them ever. The matrices come along with a pointer to a GPU routine in
CMDRAM (in master-SH-4-address-space-format), which, guess what?, draws the
player character. I *think* they specify additional transformations to be
applied to the skater body parts, but I don't know yet how.


